nyra, cunningly disguised as nyra@gmx.net wrote:

> I'm not much in favour of instant-killing. Vorpal Blade performs
> instant kills only one time in twenty (its 100% beheading chance
> against Jabberwocks is a literary reference), and only against things
> with a head. The Tsurugi has a 1/20 chance of an instant kill against
> things that are no more than human sized (does double damage against
> larger things) or an engulfing creature. It's a special power and
> shouldn't be thrown around like confetti, esp. not in the shape of
> 100% instant-kill chances against whole cohorts of monsters.

Then give it x3 damage against the targeted class of monsters, plus a 1/10
chance of destroying it outright.

You could add some extra protective effects when wielded (like with
werebane or demonbane, disabling some of the special attacks), and when
the final blow is dealt with that particular weapon (like trolls not
leaving a corpse, for example).

>>> Ogresmasher: kills ogres instantly

> I think any extras against ogres are utterly insignificant. Even
> barbarians don't need any help against ogres, and they're the only
> ones who are likely to meet significant numbers of them. I only vote
> for no instant kills because i don't want too much instakilling.

If the class of monsters is small enough, I don't see any problems with
100%-chance instant killing.

> You're considered a carnivore while wielding it and don't suffer
> cannibalism penalties; a Strength bonus sounds nice, but i fear it'd
> be too good.

Why would your god treat you differently because you're wielding a certain
weapon? Morality doesn't change by wielding a certain weapon. I agree with
the strength bonus being too strong. Extra effects should concern only
ogres.

>>> Grimtooth (I forget this even exists sometimes): always poisoned
>> (Not sure what poisoning does to monsters...)
> Something like 1d6 extra damage _and_ a 1/10 chance of instant death,
> unless the monster is poison-resistant. Nasty ... i like the idea.

So do I. Of course, the usual penalties should apply for classes that
aren't allowed to use poisoned weapons, like Samurai.

